WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:05.140
A great beginning is important, but it isn't
everything — and that definitely applies

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.140
to the movies.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.480
Plenty of films get off the starting block
just fine, but sometimes those interesting

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.710
opening acts are just a prelude to a series
of stumbles that end in utter collapse.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.110
Here's our list of movies with great beginnings...that
fell apart after minutes.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.110
Jonah Hex

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.160
Who doesn't love a sci-fi Western?

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.310
It's a genre that keeps on giving where the
small screen is concerned.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.490
Consider the lasting popularity of TV shows
like Firefly and Westworld.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:36.300
The full-on disaster Wild Wild West made it
seem like a good sci-fi western might never

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.020
make it on the silver screen...but Jonah Hex
had a shot at turning things around.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.650
Its comic book origin provided rich source
material about a troubled antihero who was

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.280
accused of being a "a turncoat and a traitor"
to his Confederate army unit.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.730
There's enough content right there for a solid
movie all on its own.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.280
However, the movie's numerous last-minute
changes dogged the film's production and turned

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.970
the final product into an unintentional disaster
movie, despite few solid intro scenes.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.600
"Searched you pretty darn good, didn't they?"

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.980
"Wouldn't you?"

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.620
The Last Airbender

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.350
M. Night Shyamalan's live-action take on The
Last Airbender should've been good.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.310
The cartoon series certainly was.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.690
But the film version was plagued by accusations
of cast white-washing, and the actors who

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:25.420
wound up in the film were misused anyway,
and largely made into movie mockeries.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.250
Pretty much the best thing about the movie
is its opening credits which show people using

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.310
tai chi to manipulate the elements.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:37.100
And that dimly lit 15-second demonstration
is as good as this movie ever gets.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.400
"Fools."

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.260
What a shame.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.900
Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.299
Let's face it.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:50.510
Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace makes
Battlefield Earth look like A New Hope running

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.130
on a crooked projector.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.120
Much has been made of the fact that Jar-Jar
Binks and a number of new aliens come across

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.030
as thinly disguised ethnic stereotypes.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.810
But bad as that definitely is, this thing
barely hangs together as an actual movie.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.680
All things considered, the movie does open
on a high note.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:12.279
The screen is black and the comforting mantra,
"A long time ago, in a galaxy far, far away…"

00:02:12.279 --> 00:02:17.109
appears onscreen, accompanied by silence,
then the classic epic fanfare.

00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:18.349
Star Wars was back!

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:19.489
After 16 years!

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:22.660
What could be a better beginning to a movie
than that?

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:27.989
The first sentence of that old, familiar Star
Wars crawl is pretty enticing, too: "Turmoil

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:29.799
has engulfed the Galactic Republic."

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:31.400
Turmoil, you say?

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.560
What sort of turmoil, Mr. Lucas?

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.879
Fans were awful excited to learn the specifics,
but then … womp.

00:02:37.879 --> 00:02:43.180
The whole thing basically turned into an intergalactic
vision of the Boston Tea Party with a crazy

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.340
costume game.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.939
"It is clear to me now that the Republic no
longer functions."

00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:48.939
No kidding.

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:53.400
Good thing The Force Awakens breathed new
life into this series by getting back to basics

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.349
because this movie — and the two prequels
that followed — were a hot mess.

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:00.439
Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:05.769
While the Wahlbergian Age of Extinction gets
a lot of hate and Dark of the Moon isn't exactly

00:03:05.769 --> 00:03:10.189
a work of art, it's not much of a stretch
to pick Revenge of the Fallen as the ugliest

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.559
of the subpar Transformers sequels.

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:17.419
It starts out in the usual course with a follow-up
to its robot action predecessor by going back

00:03:17.419 --> 00:03:20.269
in time to the start of these robotic renegades.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:24.840
But then things start to fall into some shady
territory with a racist robo-thread running

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.029
through the entirety of this itchy CGI sweater
of cinematic sin that, if pulled, causes Bay's

00:03:31.029 --> 00:03:33.409
movie to completely collapse.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:37.760
From the unflattering characteristics of its
stereotypically primitive tribesmen to the

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.200
minstrelsy of the Twins Mudflap and Skids,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.240
"What are we going to do with this shrimp
taco?"

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.800
"Let's just pop a cap in his ass, throw him
in the trunk, and nobody gonna know nothing,

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:45.300
know what I mean?"

00:03:45.309 --> 00:03:50.470
to the pervasive racially tinged one-liners,
Revenge of the Fallen retrofits a beloved

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:56.620
sci-fi franchise with deplorable racist tropes
— and then only gets worse from there.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.430
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.060
Director Zack Snyder has some explaining to
do.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.340
The widely derided Batman v Superman: Dawn
of Justice wasn't his first superhero movie,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.419
so there's no excuse for why he got this one
so wrong.

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:15.749
Putting Batman and Superman together for the
very first time in a live-action movie was

00:04:15.749 --> 00:04:17.370
a sure thing by any measure.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:21.580
Then, the fact that Wonder Woman was also
added to the mix, made half the blockbuster

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.020
battle won even without a script.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.380
And that's kinda how Dawn of Justice met its
downfall, really.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.280
Instead of a sensible narrative, it's just
a collection of stuff that happens, much of

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.960
which doesn't make a lick of sense if you
spend more than a couple of seconds thinking

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:35.960
about it.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.840
Even the rock 'em sock 'em fun inherent in
the title gets steamrolled by a grim, loud,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:45.520
and utterly preposterous final showdown between
our two main heroes that's ultimately settled

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.400
because both of their moms are named Martha.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.400
Whoo.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.390
And things started off pretty well.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.090
Sure, there was the Terrible Poetry Corner,
courtesy of Ben Affleck:

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.000
"There was a time above, a time before.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.780
There were perfect things."

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:06.630
But once we get to Bruce Wayne, zipping around
Metropolis as it's being turned into a smoking

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:11.510
crater thanks to Superman and Zod, it's tough
not to get excited about the high-stakes drama

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:12.510
to come.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:13.510
And then?

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:14.510
Well…

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.700
"She gave me faith that there's good in this
world.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:20.900
She was my world."

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:26.720
So yeah, in terms of concept, brand legacy,
and overall anticipation, Batman v Superman:

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.370
Dawn of Justice got off to an excellent start
— and then the rest of the movie showed

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:32.370
up.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:33.370
Thanks for watching!

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.680
